%[text] # ACMFDS — PROJECT CODE AND TECHNICAL REPORT
%[text] Adaptive Context-Aware Multi-Objective Flight Decision System (ACMFDS) Autonomous quadrotor simulation using Python, MuJoCo, PySide6, NumPy and scikit-learn.
%[text] REPORT PURPOSE This report documents the actual implementation in the ACMFDS\_Orbit\_Expanded project. It focuses on architecture, algorithms, decision logic, key code paths, adjustable environmental conditions, obstacle editing, telemetry, validation and limitations. Python snippets below are source references: they are not MATLAB code and should not be executed as MATLAB statements. The complete executable implementation is in the companion ACMFDS\_Orbit\_Expanded project source bundle.
%[text] ## 1. PROJECT DEFINITION
%[text] ACMFDS is a simulated autonomous-quadrotor decision and navigation system. It joins: (1) a 3D physics world provided by MuJoCo, (2) a collision-aware grid path planner, (3) a lightweight data-driven obstacle-risk estimator, (4) adaptive weights for multiple flight objectives, (5) a feedback controller with wind and battery policies, and (6) a PySide6 dashboard for observation and interactive scenario editing.
%[text] The simulation mission begins at START = (0, 0, 2) metres and uses the configured destination (12, 10, 2) metres. The destination can be moved at run time. The arena contains static obstacles, moving dynamic obstacles and building-like visual geometry.
%[text] MAIN PROBLEM A shortest geometric route is not always a useful route. A route should also avoid obstacles with clearance, remain trackable, limit excessive turning, consider energy and time costs, and be recalculated when the environment or the goal changes.
%[text] PROJECT OBJECTIVES 1. Generate safe 2D XY paths through an obstacle-filled arena. 2. Produce several route profiles and rank them with a multi-objective cost. 3. Estimate relative obstacle risk from distance, speed, battery and tracking error. 4. Adjust objective weights as risk, remaining energy, distance and speed change. 5. Simulate a quadrotor in MuJoCo and follow path waypoints using feedback control. 6. Allow customization of static and dynamic obstacles and the destination. 7. Apply simulated wind and battery conditions to speed, risk and mission decisions. 8. Expose state, mission progress, risk, weights, force, battery and plots in a GUI.
%[text] SCOPE AND SAFETY This is a research/teaching simulation demonstration, not a certified aircraft controller. GPS values are simulated, the Random Forest is trained on synthetic data, and wind, battery and return/landing rules are simplified model policies. Do not connect these policies to a physical drone without independent engineering, validation and safeguards.
%[text] ## 2. SOFTWARE ARCHITECTURE
%[text] app.py Creates the Qt application, instantiates QuadrotorSimulation and opens Dashboard.
%[text] simulation.py Owns the MuJoCo model and data, the quadrotor state, controller, battery, wind force, obstacle state, route following, dynamic obstacle motion, landing policies and telemetry.
%[text] planner.py Implements obstacle geometry, clearance, collision checks, A\* grid search, alternative route families, route simplification, route metrics, multi-objective scoring and ranking.
%[text] data\_model.py Trains a RandomForestRegressor on repeatable synthetic risk examples and calculates normalized adaptive Safety/Energy/Time/Tracking/Mission weights.
%[text] dashboard.py Builds the PySide6 interface: 3D MuJoCo viewer, top-view XY map, drag handles, obstacle customization, goal editing, route profiles, wind/battery inputs, status cards and charts.
%[text] config.py Defines start and destination, simulation frequency, starting battery and the simulated local-XY-to-latitude/longitude coordinate conversion.
%[text] requirements.txt Lists NumPy, scikit-learn, MuJoCo and PySide6 dependencies.
%[text] run\_mac.sh / run\_windows.bat Platform launchers for starting the Python application with its dependencies.
%[text] DATA FLOW Dashboard input -\> simulation state update -\> active obstacle list -\> path planner -\> route ranking -\> selected waypoints -\> feedback controller -\> MuJoCo physics step -\> telemetry/risk/weights update -\> dashboard refresh. Dynamic obstacles can trigger periodic replanning; edits to obstacles or destination request a route update after editing ends.
%[text] ## 3. WORLD MODEL AND COORDINATE SYSTEM
%[text] The planner works in local XY coordinates measured in metres. X represents east and Y represents north in the simulated ENU-like frame. Z is altitude. Planner search limits and grid resolution are set in planner.py:
%[text] RES = 0.25; % grid-cell spacing, in metres MINX = -2.0; MINY = -2.0; MAXX = 14.5; MAXY = 12.5; MARGIN = 0.55; % inflated safety margin around rectangular obstacles
%[text] Static planner obstacles are represented as tuples: (centre\_x, centre\_y, half\_width, half\_depth, height). Dynamic obstacle specifications add: (axis, amplitude\_m, angular\_rate\_rad\_s, phase\_rad).
%[text] The MuJoCo world is generated from MJCF model text in simulation.py. A named free joint gives the drone position and velocity state. Named geometry/body IDs allow the program to change target, obstacles, dynamic bodies and path markers without rebuilding the world.
%[text] Important separation: the planner uses the active obstacle geometry for route safety, while MuJoCo provides physical state evolution and a rendered 3D view. When obstacle positions are edited, both representations must be updated together; otherwise the route could be planned around a location that does not match the visible world.
%[text] ## 4. COLLISION GEOMETRY AND CLEARANCE
%[text] planner.py represents each static obstacle as an axis-aligned rectangle in XY. For a query point (x,y) and obstacle centre (ox,oy), half dimensions (hx,hy), the distance to the rectangle boundary region is computed from the non-negative outside offsets:
%[text] dx = max(abs(x - ox) - hx, 0) dy = max(abs(y - oy) - hy, 0) distance = sqrt(dx^2 + dy^2)
%[text] obstacle\_distance returns the minimum distance over all active obstacle rectangles. The blocked test inflates each rectangle by MARGIN in X and Y. A grid point inside this inflated region is considered unavailable to the planner.
%[text] Key source reference — planner.py: def blocked(x, y, obstacles=None): obstacles = OBSTACLES if obstacles is None else obstacles return any(abs(x-ox) \<= hx+MARGIN and abs(y-oy) \<= hy+MARGIN for ox, oy, hx, hy, \_ in obstacles)
%[text] path\_is\_safe does not only test path vertices. It samples each connecting segment at short intervals and checks every sample against the inflated obstacles. This matters because two safe endpoints could be connected by a straight line that clips an obstacle.
%[text] Diagonal grid moves receive an extra check: both adjacent orthogonal cells must be free and the swept segment must also be safe. This prevents diagonal corner-cutting around an obstacle. The path-simplification stage uses line-of-sight checks before skipping points.
%[text] ## 5. A\* GRID PATH PLANNING
%[text] plan\_xy(start, goal, obstacles, clearance\_weight, preference) performs A\* search.
%[text] A. Discretization Continuous XY coordinates are converted to grid indices using RES = 0.25 m. A grid index can be converted back to local metres. If the requested start or goal falls inside an inflated obstacle, \_nearest\_valid searches nearby cells for a valid cell.
%[text] B. Neighbour expansion Each grid node considers up to eight neighbours: horizontal, vertical and diagonal moves. Nodes outside the arena or within an inflated obstacle are rejected. Diagonal movements are also checked for corner cutting and for a safe segment.
%[text] C. Search cost Each candidate move combines base movement cost, clearance penalty and route-family bias.
%[text] move\_cost = hypot(dx, dy) safety\_cost = clearance\_weight / max(clearance, 0.20) family\_cost = 0.055 \* abs(y - preferred\_y) new\_cost = previous\_cost + move\_cost + safety\_cost + family\_cost
%[text] The A\* priority is new\_cost plus a Euclidean distance heuristic to the goal. The safety penalty makes cells close to obstacles more expensive; it does not replace collision checks.
%[text] D. Alternative route families generate\_candidate\_paths requests route preferences named LOWER, LOWER-MID, SLIGHT-LOW, BALANCED, SLIGHT-HIGH, UPPER-MID and UPPER. A sinusoidal offset from the direct line creates different route families while keeping start and end points aligned. Invalid or duplicate paths are excluded.
%[text] E. Simplification and validation After reconstructing the path from parent links, the planner greedily tries to connect the current point to the farthest later point that remains collision-free. This reduces turns and waypoints without discarding safety tests.
%[text] Failure mode: if no collision-free path is found, the planner raises an exception. The simulation records a planning warning and keeps the dashboard available; any fallback path must be treated as a visible diagnostic condition, not as proof of safety.
%[text] ## 6. MULTI-OBJECTIVE ROUTE EVALUATION
%[text] evaluate\_path computes route length, minimum clearance, turning penalty and normalized Safety, Energy, Time, Tracking and Mission scores. Values are designed for scoring and comparison, not calibrated physical units.
%[text] Route length = sum of Euclidean distances between consecutive path points. Minimum clearance = smallest sampled distance from the path to an active obstacle. Safety score = 1 - clipped(clearance / 2.5). Energy score = clipped(length / 16). Estimated time = length / max(speed, 0.8). Time score = clipped(estimated\_time / 14). Tracking score = normalized accumulated angular turn penalty. Mission score = clipped((length - direct\_distance) / direct\_distance).
%[text] Overall route score: score = w\_safety\*Safety + w\_energy\*Energy + w\_time\*Time + w\_tracking\*Tracking + w\_mission\*Mission
%[text] Lower score is better. rank\_paths evaluates all generated candidate routes, sorts first by score, then by greater clearance, then by shorter length, and assigns ranks. The dashboard allows a user to select a route profile and request it from the drone's current position.
%[text] Design note: route metrics are heuristic and the weights are model policy parameters. They provide consistent comparison in this simulation; they are not empirically calibrated flight performance or a formal guarantee that a selected path is globally optimal in 3D space.
%[text] ## 7. DATA-DRIVEN RISK ESTIMATION
%[text] DataDrivenRiskModel in data\_model.py uses scikit-learn RandomForestRegressor. It is trained on 2,500 reproducible synthetic examples generated using NumPy's seeded random generator.
%[text] Input features: 1. nearest obstacle clearance (metres) 2. current speed (m/s) 3. battery percentage 4. normalized waypoint tracking error
%[text] Synthetic target-risk relationship used by the training set: risk\_target = 0.62\*exp(-nearest/1.15) + 0.16\*clip(speed/2.6,0,1) + 0.12\*(1 - battery/100) + 0.10\*tracking\_error
%[text] Random noise is added and labels are clipped to \[0,1\]. The model uses 80 trees, maximum depth 8 and fixed random\_state=42 for reproducibility. predict() builds a feature row and clips the predicted result to \[0,1\].
%[text] Critical interpretation: this model demonstrates a data-driven component, but its training labels are synthetic. It does not establish real-world predictive validity. A research version should train and evaluate on representative labelled simulation logs or flight-risk data, use held-out validation, report metrics, and check distribution shift.
%[text] ## 8. ADAPTIVE OBJECTIVE WEIGHTS
%[text] adaptive\_weights(risk,battery,distance,speed,remaining\_time) creates five non-negative raw weights, then divides each by their sum so all weights total 1.
%[text] Raw safety = 0.18 + 0.48\*clip(risk, 0, 1) Raw energy = 0.12 + 0.30\*(1 - clip(battery/100, 0, 1)) Raw time = 0.07 + 0.18\*clip(distance/18, 0, 1) Raw tracking= 0.14 + 0.18\*clip(speed/2.4, 0, 1) Raw mission = 0.14 + 0.22\*clip(remaining\_time/150, 0, 1)
%[text] For each objective i, normalized\_weight\_i = raw\_weight\_i / sum(raw\_weights).
%[text] The live simulation also raises the energy weight when battery is low or wind is strong, then renormalizes the complete vector. The dashboard visualizes these weights as bars. Interpretation: - High risk raises the relative priority of Safety. - Low battery increases Energy emphasis. - Larger remaining distance can increase Time emphasis. - Higher speed can increase Tracking emphasis. - Remaining mission time affects Mission emphasis.
%[text] These equations are explicit heuristics surrounding the learned risk estimate. They are not a reinforcement-learning policy and do not automatically learn from flight outcomes.
%[text] ## 9. MUJOCO QUADROTOR SIMULATION AND CONTROLLER
%[text] QuadrotorSimulation owns the MuJoCo MjModel and MjData objects. The model timestep is set to 1/SIM\_HZ; the configured simulation frequency is 60 Hz. On each step the controller reads position and velocity, advances the active waypoint, computes position error and produces a force command. MuJoCo integrates the forces and updates the simulated state.
%[text] High-level control sequence in simulation.py: 1. Read drone position and velocity from MuJoCo state arrays. 2. Move waypoint index forward once current waypoint error is below 0.36 m. 3. Calculate speed scaling from wind and battery. 4. Convert waypoint error into desired velocity; clamp horizontal and vertical limits. 5. Apply proportional velocity feedback: acceleration = 2.8\*(desired\_velocity - velocity). 6. Clamp acceleration to avoid excessive command values. 7. Convert acceleration to force and add a wind disturbance force. 8. Call mujoco.mj\_step, then update dynamic hazards, battery, telemetry and history. 9. Replan around moving obstacles at a 3-second interval when enabled. 10. Evaluate return-home, emergency-landing and target-reached conditions.
%[text] Core code excerpt (Python reference): wind\_scale = max(0.62, 1.0 - 0.032 \* wind\_speed\_mps) battery\_scale = 1.0 if battery \>= 35.0 else (0.62 + 0.38\*battery/35.0) speed\_scale = min(wind\_scale, battery\_scale) desired\_velocity = 1.65 \* speed\_scale \* error acceleration = 2.8 \* (desired\_velocity - velocity)
%[text] Wind is expressed in world-frame direction, with 0 degrees along +X and 90 degrees along +Y. The simulated horizontal wind force is proportional to wind speed: wind\_accel = 0.18 \* wind\_speed\_mps wind\_force = drone\_mass \* wind\_accel \* \[cos(direction), sin(direction), 0\]
%[text] Total controller and wind force is applied through MuJoCo's external body-force array. This is a simplified force-based demonstration, not a high-fidelity propulsion model.
%[text] ## 10. BATTERY MODEL AND CONDITION-BASED DECISIONS
%[text] The battery state is a simulated percentage. It falls on every physics step according to a base speed-dependent draw and a wind multiplier:
%[text] speed\_cost = 0.48 + 0.14\*min(speed/1.6, 1) wind\_cost\_multiplier = 1 + 0.025\*(wind\_speed\_mps^1.25) battery = max(0, battery - speed\_cost\*wind\_cost\_multiplier\*dt)
%[text] Wind/battery user controls accept wind speed 0-12 m/s, wind direction 0-359 degrees and battery 0-100%. Applying a new condition updates the active values, telemetry and route.
%[text] Return-home threshold: return\_home\_threshold = min(43, 25 + 1.5\*wind\_speed\_mps) percent
%[text] Decision policies: - Battery at or below 8%: start simulated emergency landing at current XY. - Otherwise, battery at or below the wind-adjusted reserve: return to the blue home/start pad. - After arriving above the home pad: command a vertical descent to the landing target. - Battery at or below 35%: reduce commanded speed and emphasize energy conservation. - Wind at or above 8 m/s: use a stabilizing/high-wind context and reduce speed. - High combined risk: decision state can become AVOID / STABILIZE.
%[text] The exact precedence is implemented in set\_flight\_conditions, \_update\_telemetry and step(). The UI reports the selected state as a decision, context, mode and phase. These are simulated policy states, not validated real-flight emergency procedures.
%[text] ## 11. STATIC AND DYNAMIC OBSTACLE CUSTOMIZATION
%[text] Static obstacles (red S1-S6): - A static obstacle stores centre X/Y, half-width, half-depth and height. - move\_static\_obstacle validates the index, clamps the centre to the arena and rejects placements too close to the current drone position or destination. - Both the MuJoCo geom position and the planner's obstacle list are updated. - A route is replanned after the user applies the position or finishes dragging.
%[text] Dynamic obstacles (orange D1-D2): - A dynamic obstacle has a track centre (base\_x,base\_y), size, height, axis, amplitude, angular rate and phase. - The current offset is amplitude\*sin(angular\_rate\*time + phase). - On an X sweep, X changes by the offset and Y stays at the track centre; on a Y sweep the opposite occurs. - configure\_dynamic\_obstacle accepts a new centre, axis, amplitude and angular rate while clamping the full sweep to the arena boundaries. - move\_dynamic\_obstacle\_to\_position moves the current marker while accounting for its instantaneous offset, so the patrol shape and phase are retained. - Dynamic hazards are updated during the simulation and trigger periodic route replanning.
%[text] Goal marker (green): - move\_destination validates arena limits, distance from the drone and obstacle clearance. - The green target geom, simulation destination and planner goal update together. - On release, replanning begins from the drone's current XY position to the updated goal.
%[text] Interactive editing pauses physics to avoid continuing along an old path while the user is moving a marker. The top-view editor maps between local world XY and screen pixels. Mouse release applies one consistent replan rather than recalculating on every drag event.
%[text] ## 12. ROUTE REPLANNING AND ACTIVE OBSTACLE FILTERING
%[text] \_active\_obstacles returns static obstacles, dynamic obstacles, or both depending on the dashboard toggles. The same active list is passed to collision checks and route ranking.
%[text] replan() uses the current drone position, current goal, active obstacles, adaptive weights, battery and speed to rank candidate routes. It attempts to preserve the selected route profile if that profile is still valid; otherwise it selects the top-ranked route. The active route is converted to 3D waypoints at the destination altitude, and path markers in MuJoCo are updated along the route.
%[text] A manual REPLAN button is provided. Route changes are also requested after obstacle edits, destination changes, wind/battery updates and periodic dynamic-obstacle updates.
%[text] ## 13. DASHBOARD DESIGN AND TELEMETRY
%[text] The PySide6 Dashboard contains: - Simulation Status: time, decision, context, mode and running/landed state. - Quadrotor State: position, velocity, speed and altitude. - Mission Progress: target coordinates, distance, progress and waypoint count. - Safety & Risk: nearest obstacle distance, scalar risk and safety status. - Energy: current battery percentage and progress bar. - Control Outputs: horizontal forces, vertical thrust and total control magnitude. - 3D MuJoCo View: orbitable camera, zoom and Overview/Top/Side presets. - Top View: start (blue), target (green), static (red) and dynamic (orange) markers, buildings and the selected path. Static/dynamic obstacles and the target can be moved with the mouse. - Route Profiles: candidate route selector, Use Route and Replan actions. - Customization controls: obstacle type, selected ID, X/Y, dynamic axis, movement range/rate. - Flight Conditions: wind speed, wind direction and battery controls. - Time-series plots: distance to target, battery and speed. - Adaptive Weights: live normalized objective weights.
%[text] The dashboard's periodic timer refreshes the simulation, telemetry and drawn state. Plot history is sampled at approximately five samples per second and limited to a rolling window. The top view's world\_to\_screen and screen\_to\_world helpers maintain the XY coordinate mapping.
%[text] ## 14. SIMULATED GPS AND COORDINATE CONVERSION
%[text] config.py provides local\_xy\_to\_latlon and latlon\_to\_local\_xy. The simulation uses an equirectangular approximation appropriate for a small local arena. Given reference origin latitude lat0 and Earth's radius R, the local-to-GPS approximation is:
%[text] latitude = origin\_lat + degrees(y / R) longitude = origin\_lon + degrees(x / (R\*cos(origin\_lat)))
%[text] X is east, Y is north. GPS-origin values are configuration defaults only. Reported latitude and longitude are calculated from simulated local XY; they are not measured by a receiver.
%[text] ## 15. KEY SOURCE FUNCTIONS — WHAT EACH ONE DOES
%[text] planner.py obstacle\_distance(x,y,obstacles): minimum distance from XY to the active rectangular hazards. blocked(x,y,obstacles): tests inflated obstacle boundaries using MARGIN. path\_is\_safe(path,obstacles): samples path segments and rejects intersections. plan\_xy(start,goal,...): A\* search, clearance and route-family costs, safe simplification. generate\_candidate\_paths(...): produces distinct lower/balanced/upper route families. evaluate\_path(...): calculates length, clearance and normalized objective scores. rank\_paths(...): sorts routes by weighted score, clearance and length. select\_best\_path(...): returns top route plus the full ranking.
%[text] data\_model.py DataDrivenRiskModel.\_\_init\_\_: creates seeded synthetic training data and fits a random forest. DataDrivenRiskModel.predict: estimates risk from four live state features. adaptive\_weights: normalizes five objectives and returns a name-to-weight dictionary.
%[text] simulation.py QuadrotorSimulation.\_\_init\_\_: constructs MuJoCo world IDs, obstacle state, risk model and mission. \_make\_initial\_plan: initializes objective weights and ranks paths. set\_flight\_conditions: applies wind/battery inputs and begins required return/landing policy. move\_static\_obstacle: validates and moves a red obstacle in planner and MuJoCo. configure\_dynamic\_obstacle: changes patrol centre/axis/range/rate with arena constraints. move\_destination: validates and moves the green goal, then optionally replans. \_update\_dynamic\_obstacles: updates orange obstacle positions from their patrol specifications. \_update\_telemetry: calculates speed, clearance, risk, adaptive weights and decision states. step: waypoint controller, wind force, MuJoCo integration, energy use and landing decisions. replan: ranks and activates routes with the latest obstacle/goal/condition data. reset: restarts the mission with selected goal and test conditions.
%[text] dashboard.py TopView: world/pixel mapping, hit-testing and drag handling for red, orange and green markers. MjView: MuJoCo rendering, manual orbit, auto-orbit and camera presets. Dashboard.apply\_obstacle\_customization: validates/applies UI settings to the simulation. Dashboard.apply\_flight\_conditions: sends current wind and battery controls to the simulation. Dashboard.update\_display: refreshes cards, values, plots and route information.
%[text] app.py main: creates QApplication, simulation and Dashboard, then starts the Qt event loop.
%[text] ## 16. REPRESENTATIVE IMPLEMENTATION EXCERPTS (PYTHON REFERENCE)
%[text] A. Random Forest risk model setup — data\_model.py self.model = RandomForestRegressor( n\_estimators=80, max\_depth=8, random\_state=seed, n\_jobs=-1, ) self.model.fit(features, labels)
%[text] B. Route collision check — planner.py for a, b in zip(path, path\[1:\]): for point in segment\_points(a, b): if blocked(point\[0\], point\[1\], obstacles): return False
%[text] C. Dynamic obstacle motion — simulation.py offset = amplitude \* sin(angular\_rate \* sim\_time + phase) if axis == "x": x, y = base\_x + offset, base\_y else: x, y = base\_x, base\_y + offset
%[text] D. Replanning after state changes — conceptual sequence updated\_state = apply\_obstacle\_or\_goal\_or\_condition\_change() active\_obstacles = \_active\_obstacles() ranking = rank\_paths(current\_position, destination, active\_obstacles, weights, battery, speed) activate\_selected\_route(ranking)
%[text] The exact production implementation is in the Python project source. These compact excerpts highlight the core mechanisms without copying every UI drawing statement into the report.
%[text] ## 17. RECOMMENDED EXPERIMENTS FOR PROJECT VALIDATION
%[text] Experiment 1 — Baseline navigation Initial conditions: wind 0 m/s, battery 100%, default obstacles. Record route, distance, speed and objective weights. Confirm the drone reaches the green target pad.
%[text] Experiment 2 — Static-obstacle edit Move one red S obstacle across the currently selected route. Apply the edit. Confirm the 3D obstacle and top-view marker agree, a route is recalculated, and its segment checks pass.
%[text] Experiment 3 — Dynamic sweep Change an orange D obstacle's centre, axis, range and angular rate. Observe patrol motion and periodic replanning. Test both X and Y sweeps, and verify the full track stays in the arena.
%[text] Experiment 4 — Destination editing Move the green goal to another clear location. Confirm the target pad and telemetry update, then check that waypoints lead to the new destination.
%[text] Experiment 5 — Wind response Compare 0 m/s against 6 m/s at 90 degrees. Then change direction to 180 degrees. Observe force output, trajectory disturbance, risk, speed and battery drain.
%[text] Experiment 6 — Energy conservation Apply battery 35% and observe speed scaling/energy weight. Reduce battery to the wind-adjusted return threshold and verify return-to-home. Apply 8% or less to examine emergency landing.
%[text] Experiment 7 — Obstacle toggles and replanning Enable only static obstacles, only dynamic obstacles, then both. Confirm the active obstacle list used by the planner matches the chosen toggles.
%[text] For each experiment, record simulation time, goal distance, minimum clearance, battery, selected route profile, score, risk and decision state. Repeat with the same conditions when comparing routes. These tests validate internal consistency, not hardware safety.
%[text] ## 18. TECHNICAL LIMITATIONS AND RESEARCH IMPROVEMENTS
%[text] 1\. Synthetic risk labels: train/validate on real or high-fidelity labelled data and report held-out metrics before making predictive claims. 2. Simplified dynamics: include better propulsion/attitude/drag modelling and tune controller gains using documented response tests. The current force controller is demonstration-grade. 3. 2D route planning: planner decisions are mainly XY. Extend to 3D occupancy or voxel search if altitude-dependent obstacles and overflight decisions are in scope. 4. Dynamic obstacles: the current planner replans periodically and against the active obstacle representation. A future version could predict obstacle trajectories over a time horizon. 5. Energy model: current battery drain is an empirical simulation equation, not an electrical battery model. Calibrate from logged power and mission data before drawing performance claims. 6. Safety: reserve thresholds, clearance margin and emergency landing are design policies, not a proof of collision avoidance. Add exhaustive unit tests, Monte Carlo testing and explicit infeasible-route handling before broader use. 7. Reproducibility: record all initial positions, obstacle tracks, random seeds, selected route, conditions and software versions with experiment outputs.
%[text] FUTURE WORK Replace synthetic training data, benchmark against Dijkstra/RRT\*/other planners, evaluate path length vs. clearance vs. energy, add predictive moving-obstacle avoidance, improve model-based control, and automatically export mission logs/plots for repeatable experiments.
%[text] ## 19. EXECUTION AND PROJECT FILE REFERENCE
%[text] Run the simulation from the root of the extracted ACMFDS\_Orbit\_Expanded project folder:
%[text] macOS terminal: bash run\_mac.sh
%[text] Python environment alternative after dependencies have been installed: source .venv/bin/activate python app.py
%[text] Main source files: app.py, simulation.py, planner.py, data\_model.py, config.py, dashboard.py, requirements.txt, run\_mac.sh and run\_windows.bat.
%[text] The companion project ZIP carries the complete executable Python code. This report describes the key implementations and function responsibilities; the project source is the authoritative line-by-line reference for running and modifying the simulation.
%[text] ## 20. CONCLUSION
%[text] ACMFDS integrates obstacle-aware A\* routing, multi-route scoring, a synthetic-data Random Forest risk estimator, adaptive objective weighting, MuJoCo physics and a PySide6 interface. Its main demonstration is closed-loop adaptation: changes to obstacles, destination, wind or battery update the simulated state, influence decisions and cause route selection/replanning. The implementation is useful for demonstrating the interaction between planning, environment, data-driven risk and dashboard controls. Its predictions and safety responses remain simulation policies and require further validation before any real-world interpretation.

